  Single canonical reader for `.aide/config/brain.aide` — parses the two-field
  YAML frontmatter (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`) and extracts the three required
  body sections by heading (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`)
  as separately-named fields, optionally applies install-time
  frontmatter-value interpolation to `mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users
  (a surface the default scaffold does not exercise), and returns a structured
  value whose consumers each pull only the section they need: the runtime
  brain tool reads prose, the brain provisioner reads the playbook and
  research hub bodies to scaffold their on-disk artifacts, and the sync verb
  reads only the frontmatter.
intent: >
  parseBrainAide is the single canonical-code module that knows the
  `brain.aide` file format. The file's body has grown from a single prose
  section to three named sections — `## Prose` (the agent-facing usage
  instructions returned verbatim by `aide_brain`), `## Playbook hub` (the
  bytes scaffolded as the coding-playbook hub artifact in the brain at
  install time), and `## Research hub` (the bytes scaffolded as the research
  hub artifact at install time) — so that every hub-related template lives
  inside the user-owned config file rather than hardcoded in package code.
  The parser is the load-bearing boundary that keeps the file format
  hand-written (the user owns it) while every downstream consumer sees the
  same typed structure with each body section addressable by name.
  Different consumers pull different sections: the runtime `aide_brain`
  tool reads `prose` only; the install service's brain provisioner reads
  `playbookHub` and `researchHub` to land the hub artifacts on disk; the
  boot reporter and `npx aidemd-mcp sync` verb read only the frontmatter.
  None of those consumers reach into the raw file format directly; they
  ask this module for the parsed result and access named fields. The
  frontmatter schema is the minimum that still has real consumers —
  exactly `name` (a label) and `mcpServerConfig` (consumed by sync).
  Every removed field (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`) is
  rejected as malformed-frontmatter so users migrate cleanly rather than
  carrying dead fields forward. The body section vocabulary is the
  parser's enumerated truth: every required section must be present and
  no unknown headings are silently tolerated, so the file format stays
  closed and reviewable. Substitution is partitioned by audience:
  frontmatter-value interpolation (`${fieldName}`) into
  `mcpServerConfig.args` is install-time work the parser performs on
  demand for sync-class consumers; the default scaffold does not
  exercise this surface (the path lives byte-inline in args), and the
  only frontmatter value resolvable today is `${name}`. The three body
  sections each return verbatim with zero substitution because their
  audiences (the agent reading prose; the install scaffolder writing
  hub artifacts to disk) are not the `.mcp.json` writer.
