      into module internals, breaking the central-types invariant."
---

## Context

Before this module exists, the package's brain layer treats the host's
`.mcp.json` `brain` entry as the authoritative source for what brain is
wired and where its data lives. After the refactor settled in the parent
intent spec, the source of truth is a hand-written file the host owns —
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, sitting under the new user-owned
`.aide/config/` scaffold-once-never-overwritten directory — and
`.mcp.json` becomes a derived artifact the host's `npx aidemd-mcp sync`
writes from that file. Four consumers all need to read the same
brain.aide and agree on what they see: the boot reporter detecting
drift, the runtime brain tool returning agent-facing prose verbatim,
the install service scaffolding fresh installs and seeding the brain's
hub artifacts, and the sync verb writing the derived `.mcp.json`
entry. Without a single parser, each consumer would re-implement YAML
reading, body section extraction, and frontmatter validation — the
exact decentralization the brain backend registry's retirement was
meant to eliminate.

The parser is therefore peer-level service code, not a helper of any
single consumer. It sits at `src/service/parseBrainAide/` alongside
`install/`, `buildBrainState/`, and (post-retirement) the deleted
`brainBackends/` slot. Two consumers in `src/service/`, one in
`src/tools/`, and one in `src/cli/` all depend on it; placing it under
any single consumer would force the others to reach across a sibling
boundary the playbook's horizontal-isolation rule forbids.

The body has grown from a single agent-facing prose section to three
named sections, and that growth is the load-bearing reason this spec
was rewritten. The framework's brain contract is exactly two required
hubs (a coding playbook hub and a research hub); previously the bytes
of those hub artifacts were embedded as inline TypeScript constants
inside the install service, which made the package the author of
content the user is supposed to own and edit. The refactor moves all
hub-template content out of inline package constants and into the
user-owned `brain.aide`, so a single canonical file holds every
agent-facing string that lands on the host's disk: the runtime prose
the agent reads through `aide_brain`, the playbook-hub artifact the
install service scaffolds in the brain on first install, and the
research-hub artifact the install service scaffolds alongside it. One
file the user owns; one parser that reads it; three consumer-facing
sections each consumed by exactly one downstream module by name. The
parser is the single canonical reader that extracts all three sections
from the same file in one parse pass.

The frontmatter schema is the parent spec's load-bearing minimum:
exactly `name` (a label) and `mcpServerConfig` (consumed by sync).
Every other field the prior schema carried — `connector`, `rootPath`,
`entryFile`, `tools` — has been removed because no runtime code
consumed it; their presence in a parsed file was validation theater.
The parser's job is to enforce that minimum: accept the two-field
shape, reject deprecated fields by name so users migrate cleanly, and
return a typed structure whose surface area matches the schema's
surface area exactly. Brain location lives once, inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args`; the parser never derives a logical root from
args, never stats a path, never introspects args to infer connector
identity. The interface contract for what MCP tools the agent calls
lives in the `## Prose` body section, not in frontmatter — the agent
reads the prose verbatim, calls whatever it names, and surfaces
failures fast.

The interpolation surface is the second load-bearing decision but is
nearly dormant by default. The parent spec partitions substitution
into two surfaces with different audiences: install-time interpolation
of `mcpServerConfig.args` (the `.mcp.json` writer's audience) and
agent-or-disk-time body sections (the agent and the install
scaffolder's audiences). The same parser knows about both surfaces
because both derive from the same parsed file, but the helper that
performs interpolation is exposed separately so consumers that don't
need it (the brain tool returning prose, the brain provisioner
scaffolding hub artifacts, the boot reporter checking drift on
disk-shape) cannot accidentally invoke it. With the schema collapsed
to `name` + `mcpServerConfig`, the only frontmatter value resolvable
by `${...}` is `${name}`, and the default scaffold contains no
placeholders at all — the helper exists for advanced users who want
to DRY a value across positions. The shape still keeps the parent
spec's bad example (server-side substitution leaking into any body
section) impossible by construction: substitution runs only on
`mcpServerConfig.args`, and never on `prose`, `playbookHub`, or
`researchHub`.

## Strategy

**Single canonical reader, four consumers.** The boot reporter, the
brain tool, the install scaffolder, and the sync verb all import from
this module. None of them re-parses YAML, none of them extracts a
body section independently, none of them validates required
frontmatter fields. Centralizing format knowledge here means a future
change to the brain.aide schema — a new section heading, a new
required frontmatter field, a tightening of the body grammar — is a
one-file edit; the consumers see the new shape automatically. This is
the same chokepoint pattern the install service's `initContent`
already uses for canonical docs — one reader, many composers.

**Three named body sections, extracted in one parse pass.** The body
grammar is a small enumerated set of section headings:
`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, and `## Research hub`. The parser
walks the body once after frontmatter validation, locates each
heading at the start of a line, and slices each section from its
heading boundary through the next known heading (or end-of-file for
the last section). The parsed result on the `ok` branch carries each
section as its own field — `prose`, `playbookHub`, `researchHub` — so
consumers reach for the section they own by name without having to
know the body grammar. The field-name shape (camelCase keys mapped
from heading-cased section titles) is the parser's translation
boundary between the file format the user reads and writes and the
typed object the consumers branch on. No consumer reaches into a
freeform body string to extract a section; if the parser exposed one,
it would have decentralized the body grammar to every consumer that
opened it.

**Strict body section vocabulary; unknown headings are
malformed-body.** The set of valid top-level body headings is exactly
`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub` — three, no more,
no less. A heading that is none of those three is a `malformed-body`
result with a reason naming the unknown heading. A required heading
that is missing is a `malformed-body` result with a reason naming
every absent section. The parser does NOT silently drop unknown
headings into a catch-all field, does NOT silently treat a missing
required section as an empty body, and does NOT case-fold the
heading text. The grammar is closed and reviewable: every accepted
heading has a typed field on the result and a downstream consumer
that owns it; everything else is a structured failure the user
resolves by editing. This is the section-level analogue of the
deprecated-fields rejection in frontmatter — both surfaces forbid
silent acceptance because silent acceptance is how a closed schema
drifts open one field at a time.

**Per-consumer section ownership.** Each section is owned by exactly
one downstream consumer, and that ownership is a contract this spec
calls out by name. The runtime brain tool reads `prose` only — never
`playbookHub`, never `researchHub`. The install service's brain
provisioner reads `playbookHub` and `researchHub` to scaffold the
hub artifacts on disk — never `prose`. The sync verb and the boot
reporter read frontmatter only — none of the body sections. A
consumer reading a section it does not own is a contract violation
even when the read is otherwise harmless: cross-section reads couple
consumers to fields they have no contract over, and the moment the
parser tightens or relaxes the grammar of a section, the wrong
consumer breaks. The named-section shape exists precisely to make
"the brain tool depends on prose, the provisioner depends on the
hubs" visible in the type signatures every consumer imports.

**Verbatim invariant generalized to every body section.** The parser
returns each body section byte-identical to what the user wrote
between its heading and the next heading boundary (or end-of-file).
No trimming, no normalization, no markdown rewriting, no template
engine. This was previously a guarantee about `## Prose` only; with
the body grown to three sections the same invariant covers all three.
A user who hand-edits their `## Playbook hub` to land a custom hub
shape on disk gets exactly those bytes scaffolded by the install
service; a user who embeds literal `${variable}` characters in
`## Research hub` because their research notes document a templating
language gets those characters preserved. The parent spec's
no-substitution-on-body-content invariant covers every section the
parser returns, not just the historically singular prose body.

**Tagged-result return shape, never throws.** The parser returns a
discriminated union: `{ kind: "ok", config, prose, playbookHub,
researchHub }`, `{ kind: "missing" }`,
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason }`,
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason }`. Every consumer branches on
`kind` and composes its own remediation prose. This mirrors how
`buildBrainState` already returns a tagged union for brain
precondition state — both modules participate in the same boot-time
diagnostic surface, and both must produce structured states the
orchestrator can speak about. Throwing would surface as an MCP
protocol error with no `instructions` field for the agent to act on,
defeating the parent spec's "structured-state at every layer"
invariant.

**Strict-failure migration policy: old prose-only files surface as
malformed-body.** A `brain.aide` carried forward from the
single-section era — frontmatter is valid, body has only `## Prose`,
no `## Playbook hub`, no `## Research hub` — returns `malformed-body`
with a reason naming the two missing sections. The parser does NOT
silently default the missing sections to empty strings, does NOT
silently scaffold them from package-side templates, and does NOT
carve out an `aide_upgrade`-time exception that auto-fills them.
The user hand-edits their file to add the new sections; the install
service ships the new default scaffold for fresh installs. Silent
defaults would re-introduce exactly the package-as-vote anti-pattern
the inline-hub-constants retirement was meant to delete: a "fill in
what the user didn't" surface where the package authors content the
user is supposed to own. Strict failure forces a deliberate
migration touch — the user knows their file changed shape because
the parser told them so. (If usage data later shows users actively
need an auto-migration carve-out, this policy is reconsidered then;
the default starts strict.)

**Substitution is exposed as a separate helper, not folded into the
parse step.** The parser returns frontmatter and every body section
verbatim. A second exported function — `interpolateArgs(config) →
string[]` — takes the parsed config and applies frontmatter-value
substitution to `mcpServerConfig.args`. Two consumers call it (the
install service's brain provisioner deriving the MCP entry, the sync
verb writing `.mcp.json`); the rest do not (the brain tool returning
prose, the brain provisioner scaffolding hub artifacts, the boot
reporter checking drift). Splitting the interpolation into an opt-in
helper means a consumer that never imports `interpolateArgs` cannot
accidentally apply substitution to something it shouldn't — none of
the body sections has any path through the exported surface that
runs interpolation on it. This closes the parent spec's bad example
about substitution leaking into agent-facing or disk-bound content.

**Interpolation is generalized over frontmatter fields, not
field-specific.** The substitution engine walks each string in `args`
looking for `${<key>}` patterns and replaces them with the matching
top-level frontmatter value. With the schema collapsed to `name` +
`mcpServerConfig`, the only resolvable placeholder today is
`${name}`; the default scaffold uses no placeholders at all and the
helper is a no-op for default installs. The engine generalizes
naturally if the schema ever grows — no field-specific privilege.
Crucially, the engine does NOT know about environment variables,
shell metacharacters, or any other expansion source — substitution
is purely frontmatter-value-to-args-position. This is small enough
to keep inline in this module without a sub-helper folder.

**`name` is metadata, never dispatched on.** The parser reads `name`
from frontmatter and includes it in the returned config (consumers
may surface it in `aide_info` for human-readable display). But no
parser code branches on its value, and different brains may share
names without affecting code behavior. If a future consumer wants
per-brain behavior, the right move is to push that decision back
into the user's `brain.aide` (where the user authors brain-specific
prose and brain-specific hub bytes verbatim), not to add a registry
to this module. The parent spec's no-connector-branching invariant
makes this load-bearing under the renamed field.

**Schema is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`;
deprecated fields are rejected, not stripped.** Required frontmatter
fields are `name`, `mcpServerConfig.command`, and
`mcpServerConfig.args`; absence of any of these returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: <which field> }`.
Presence of any removed field — `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
`tools` — also returns `malformed-frontmatter` with a reason that
names every stale field it found. The parser does NOT silently strip
deprecated fields; rejection forces clean migration rather than
indefinite drift. Defaults are never injected for missing required
fields — no defaulting `name: obsidian`, no defaulting
`mcpServerConfig.command: npx`. A missing required field is a user
error to surface, not a hole to paper over. The user's edit is
authoritative; package-side defaults would re-introduce the "vote
the package gets to override" pattern the parent spec's bad examples
explicitly rule out.

**Types live centrally in `src/types/`, not module-private.** The
`BrainAideConfig` shape (`{ name; mcpServerConfig: { command; args }
}`), the `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` slice, and the
`ParseBrainAideResult` tagged union — including the three body
section fields on the `ok` variant — all live in `src/types/index.ts`
and are imported by every consumer. The parser does not export its
own type definitions for the parsed shape. This keeps the consumers
on a shared central type surface so a schema change is one edit in
one file, with type errors propagating to every downstream module
that depends on the changed shape. A consumer that has to
`import type { ParsedBrainAide } from '@/service/parseBrainAide'` is
reaching into module internals; the central-types invariant exists
to make that import impossible.

**Single-helper file layout.** The module has one orchestrator export
(`parseBrainAide`), one auxiliary export (`interpolateArgs`), and an
exported in-memory string parser sibling (`parseBrainAideFromString`)
that the install service uses to derive the MCP entry from
template bytes that have not been written to disk yet. All three fit
comfortably under ~200 lines combined and do not warrant separate
sub-helper folders. The progressive-disclosure "single-helper
exception" applies — they stay in one `index.ts`, with helpers
defined above the default export to honor the function-ordering
rule. When the file format grows (a new tagged-result variant, a new
section, environment-variable-style expansions), factor each
responsibility into its own subfolder.

**Pure detection, no I/O orchestration of `.mcp.json`.** The parser
reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` and parses it. It does not stat any
path embedded in args (path validation is the launcher's job at MCP
server startup), it does not write `.mcp.json` (that's `sync`'s job),
it does not write hub artifacts to disk (that's the install service's
apply layer's job), it does not enumerate the file system. One async
hop (`readFile`) on the happy path. This keeps the module's test
surface tiny and makes the consumers' tests independent — the brain
tool's tests don't have to mock filesystem walks the parser doesn't
perform.

## Good examples

**A complete three-section `brain.aide` parses to the expected
structure.** The host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` contains:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "D:/notes/my-vault"
---

## Prose

Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open
files by their vault-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes`
for keyword queries across every note in the vault. The vault's
entry file is `CLAUDE.md` at the vault root — read that first; it
carries wikilinks (`[[note-name]]`) you follow to deepen context.

## Playbook hub

# Coding playbook

A hub for coding conventions, patterns, and architecture decisions.
Read this index first, then drill into the section that matches your
task.

## Task routing

| Task type | Read first |
| --------- | ---------- |
|           |            |

## Sections

(Add sections here as your playbook grows.)

## Research hub

# Research

A hub for domain research notes. Each subdirectory holds notes for a
single domain; read the domain hub before drilling into individual
notes.

## Domains

(Add domain hubs here as your research grows.)
```

The parser returns:

```
{
  kind: "ok",
  config: {
    name: "obsidian",
    mcpServerConfig: {
      command: "npx",
      args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-vault"]
    }
  },
  prose: "Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use `mcp__brain__read_note`...",
  playbookHub: "# Coding playbook\n\nA hub for coding conventions...",
  researchHub: "# Research\n\nA hub for domain research notes..."
}
```

No `connector`, no `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools` appear in
the parsed shape — the schema is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig`.
The `args` array carries the path inline byte-for-byte under user
ownership; no placeholders, no substitution. Each of `prose`,
`playbookHub`, and `researchHub` starts immediately after its
heading line and ends at the next heading boundary (or end-of-file
for the last section), byte-identical to what the user wrote between
those bounds. Which MCP tools the agent calls
(`mcp__brain__read_note`, `mcp__brain__search_notes`) appears only
in the `## Prose` section — the agent reads them there, not from a
frontmatter declaration.

**Each consumer pulls only the section it owns.** Given the parsed
result above, the four downstream consumers reach for different
fields and never cross over:

- `aide_brain` (the runtime brain tool) reads `result.prose`
  verbatim and returns it as `instructions`. It never touches
  `result.playbookHub` or `result.researchHub` — those sections do
  not exist as far as this consumer's contract is concerned.
- `provisionBrain` (the install service's brain provisioner) reads
  `result.playbookHub` to source the bytes of the playbook-hub
  artifact InitStep and `result.researchHub` to source the bytes of
  the research-hub artifact InitStep. It never touches
  `result.prose` — agent-facing instructions are not the
  provisioner's concern. It also calls `interpolateArgs(result.config)`
  to derive the MCP step's args.
- `cli/sync` reads `result.config.mcpServerConfig` (after
  `interpolateArgs` runs against args), writes the resulting entry
  into `.mcp.json` under `mcpServers.brain`, and ignores every body
  section.
- The boot reporter reads `result.config.mcpServerConfig` (again
  through `interpolateArgs`) to compare against the actual entry in
  `.mcp.json` for drift detection. Body sections are irrelevant to
  the comparison.

The named-section shape makes those ownership lines visible in the
type signatures: a consumer reaching outside its owned slice imports
a field its module's `.aide` spec does not authorize.

**Sync calls the interpolation helper; on the default scaffold it is
a no-op.** Given the parsed config above:

```
interpolateArgs(config) === ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-vault"]
```

The args contain no `${...}` placeholders, so the helper returns
them unchanged. Sync writes the result into `.mcp.json` under
`mcpServers.brain` along with `command: "npx"`. The brain tool
returning the prose body for this same config never calls
`interpolateArgs` — it returns `result.prose` verbatim. The
provisioner scaffolding the playbook-hub and research-hub artifacts
also never calls `interpolateArgs` against those bytes; they go to
disk verbatim.

**An advanced user opts into interpolation by referencing `${name}`
in args.** A user authoring a brain whose launcher accepts the
brain name as a CLI arg writes:

```
---
name: my-vault
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "some-launcher"
    - "--profile"
    - "${name}"
---
```

(plus the three required body sections, unchanged from above.) The
parser returns the args verbatim with `"${name}"` literal.
`interpolateArgs(config)` substitutes the `${name}` placeholder
against the matching top-level frontmatter field, returning
`["some-launcher", "--profile", "my-vault"]`. The substitution is
purely frontmatter-value-to-args-position; the engine knows nothing
about environment variables or shell expansion. The body sections
are returned untouched even if they contain literal `${name}`
characters in their text — interpolation never reaches any body
section.

**A missing `.aide/config/brain.aide` returns `{ kind: "missing" }`.**
A fresh project that has not run `aide_init` yet has no `brain.aide`
on disk. The parser tries to read it, gets ENOENT, and returns
`{ kind: "missing" }`. The boot reporter forwards this to
`aide_info.brain.status === "no-brain-aide"`. The orchestrator
HALTS and tells the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init`.

**A frontmatter missing `name` returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "name is required" }`.**
The user hand-edited `brain.aide` and accidentally deleted the
`name` line. The parser reads YAML, validates required fields,
detects the absence, and returns a structured failure. No defaulting
to "obsidian" or any other label — the user's broken edit is
surfaced as a structured state, and the orchestrator routes them to
fix their file. The same shape applies to a missing
`mcpServerConfig.command` or `mcpServerConfig.args`.

**A frontmatter carrying deprecated fields returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "deprecated fields:
rootPath, tools" }`.** A user upgrading from a pre-collapse
`brain.aide` runs the parser against their old file. The parser
reads YAML, sees `rootPath` and `tools`, and returns a structured
failure naming every stale field. The user removes them, moves the
path inline into `mcpServerConfig.args`, and reruns sync. Clean
migration; no silent acceptance carrying dead fields forward into
a file the package has stopped honoring.

**A pre-widening prose-only `brain.aide` returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "missing required sections:
## Playbook hub, ## Research hub" }`.** A user whose `brain.aide`
predates the body widening still has a single `## Prose` section
and nothing else. The parser validates the frontmatter (which is
unchanged shape), walks the body, finds `## Prose` but neither of
the two new headings, and returns the structured failure. The user
adds the two missing sections by hand (or copies the new defaults
from the bundled scaffold helper) and reruns. No silent default
fills in the missing bytes; the user's deliberate edit is the only
path that resolves the failure.

**A `brain.aide` with an unknown body heading returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "unknown heading: ## Notes" }`.**
A user adds a `## Notes` section to keep some scratch text inside
their `brain.aide`. The parser walks the body, finds the unknown
heading, and returns the structured failure. The body grammar is
closed; users keep scratch text in a sibling file instead. The
parser does not silently drop the unknown section into a catch-all
field.

## Bad examples

**A parser that silently accepts unknown body headings.** The user
adds a `## Notes` section to their `brain.aide` for scratch text.
The parser walks the body, finds the unknown heading, and either
drops it into a catch-all `extras` field on the result, attaches
the bytes to the nearest known section (so `## Prose` swells with
the unrelated `## Notes` content), or simply ignores the section
without telling anyone. Now the body grammar drifts open one
heading at a time while the parser pretends the schema is still
closed; the next user adds a `## Prompts` section, the one after
adds `## Tasks`, and within a few releases every `brain.aide` in
the wild has a different shape. The fix is the structured failure:
unknown headings are `malformed-body` with a reason naming the
unknown heading, full stop.

**A parser that auto-fills missing required body sections from
package defaults.** The user's `brain.aide` predates the body
widening — only `## Prose` is present. The parser detects the
missing `## Playbook hub` and `## Research hub`, looks them up in
a package-side template constant, and returns the parsed result
with package-authored bytes substituted in for the missing
sections. The install service now scaffolds package-authored
playbook and research hubs onto the user's disk every time they
run init or upgrade, even though the user's deliberate file shape
said neither section should exist yet. This re-introduces exactly
the package-as-vote anti-pattern that the move-of-hub-content-out-
of-inline-constants was meant to delete: the package gets to fill
in content the user is supposed to own. Strict failure is the only
shape that keeps the user authoritative — the parser names the
missing sections, the user adds them, no package-side bytes leak
into the user-owned file.

**A consumer reading another consumer's section.** The boot reporter
imports `result.playbookHub` "to validate that the section is
non-empty" or "to surface a preview in the boot status." The brain
tool concatenates `result.config.name` plus `result.prose` plus
`result.researchHub` "for richer agent context." Each consumer's
section ownership is part of its contract; reaching outside that
contract couples the consumer to a section it has no authority
over, and the moment the parser tightens or relaxes the grammar of
the unowned section, the wrong consumer breaks. The runtime brain
tool reads `prose` only; the brain provisioner reads `playbookHub`
and `researchHub` only; the sync verb and boot reporter read
frontmatter only.

**A parser that returns the body as a single freeform string.**
"The consumers can extract sections themselves with a regex; the
parser shouldn't be in the markdown-splitting business." Now every
consumer of `brain.aide` carries its own copy of the body grammar,
and the parser has decentralized the format it exists to centralize.
The first consumer that miscounts a heading boundary scaffolds a
hub artifact whose bytes start mid-section; the second consumer
that uses a different regex disagrees about which bytes belong to
`## Prose`; the parser pretends the body is opaque while every
consumer pretends the body is structured. The parser owns body
section extraction; consumers reach for typed fields by name.

**Module-private type definitions for the parsed shape.** The parser
declares its own `interface ParsedBrainAide { ... }` inside
`src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` and exports it for consumers
to import. Now the parsed shape is defined twice — once in the
parser module, once implicitly in every consumer that imports the
module-internal type. A schema change requires touching each
consumer's import path, type errors no longer propagate cleanly
because the central type surface is fragmented, and the
`BrainAideConfig` type that already lives in `src/types/index.ts`
sits next to a parallel definition no one knows is canonical. The
central-types invariant exists to make exactly this fragmentation
impossible — the parsed shape lives in `src/types/index.ts` and
every consumer imports from there.

**A parser that branches on `name`.** The parser reads
`config.name === "obsidian"` and applies different validation rules
for `mcpServerConfig.args` (`obsidian` expects a path arg, `notion`
expects an API token arg, etc.) — or worse, applies different
section-extraction rules for the body ("obsidian brains have a
`## Wikilink protocol` section, others don't"). This re-introduces
the identity-inference anti-pattern the brainBackends retirement was
specifically meant to delete — different brains may share `name`
values, and the package never branches on the label. If a future
user wants brain-specific behavior, they encode it in their
hand-written body sections, not in a registry the package owns.

**A parser that silently strips deprecated fields.** The user's old
`brain.aide` still carries `connector: obsidian`, `rootPath:
D:/notes`, and `tools.read`. The parser sees them, drops them on the
floor, and returns a config containing only `name` and
`mcpServerConfig`. The user believes their file is honored — their
`tools.read` declaration is sitting right there in the file — but
nothing in the package reads it. Months later they wonder why
changing `tools.read` has no effect. Silent acceptance is the worst
possible failure mode here: rejection forces clean migration, while
silent stripping creates dead-field drift the user never finds out
about. The parser must reject deprecated fields by name.

**A parser that introspects `mcpServerConfig.args` to derive a
`rootPath`.** "We removed the `rootPath` field, but we still need to
know the vault path for the boot reporter, so let's just take
`args[1]` as the rootPath." Now the package has a structural
assumption about argument ordering that varies per launcher, and
the moment a user runs `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` with `--config-file
foo.json` instead of an inline path, the introspection reads
`foo.json` as the vault path and the boot reporter reports a
nonsensical state. Path validation is the launcher's job at MCP
server startup; the package does not second-guess it. Reading args
for any purpose other than passing them through to `.mcp.json` is
the same regression as a `rootPath` field in different clothing.

**A parser that adds an `entryFile` field back to the schema "for
documentation."** Someone notices the `## Prose` body keeps
mentioning `CLAUDE.md` as the entry point and proposes a
frontmatter `entryFile` field "so it's structured." No code reads
the field; the agent still picks up the entry path from the prose
body. This is validation theater — a frontmatter field whose only
consumer is the parser's own validation. The schema must justify
each field by naming a real runtime consumer (sync writes
`mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label).
Anything else belongs in the body sections or nowhere.

**A parser that interpolates any body section.** Before returning
results, the parser walks `prose`, `playbookHub`, or `researchHub`
and replaces `${name}` occurrences with the resolved label. The
first user whose prose legitimately documents a `${variable}`
syntax has their body mangled; the first playbook-hub that
legitimately contains a `${...}` template example gets rewritten
before reaching disk; the first research-hub note about templating
languages has its examples corrupted. Worse: every section author
now needs to know which placeholders the package will rewrite
before delivery, and the temptation to "just template the body
sections too" lands. Substitution lives only in
`mcpServerConfig.args`; every body section returns verbatim.

**A parser that throws on missing brain.aide.** The host project
calls the parser, no file exists, the parser raises an exception,
the consumer's MCP-protocol response is an error with no
`instructions` field. The orchestrator cannot tell the user what
to do because no structured state was produced. Missing-file is a
normal pre-init state; it must be a tagged-result variant.

**A parser that injects defaults for missing frontmatter fields.**
The user's `brain.aide` is missing `name`. The parser silently
defaults it to `"obsidian"` because "every brain we've scaffolded
so far is Obsidian." Three failure modes follow. First: a brain
that genuinely is not Obsidian gets the wrong label injected, and
`aide_info` narrates a label the user did not choose. Second: the
user's edit was an in-progress draft; defaulting it forward
silently completes a config the user wasn't ready to commit. Third:
the next time the user re-edits `brain.aide`, they expect the file
they wrote to round-trip through the parser; defaulting makes that
promise false. Required fields are required, and a missing required
field is a structured failure the user resolves by editing.

**A consumer that re-reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` directly
instead of calling this parser.** The boot reporter implements its
own YAML read because "we only need `mcpServerConfig` to compare
against `.mcp.json`, the rest is ceremony." Two consumers now
decode the file format, and the moment the format gains a new
section, a new required frontmatter field, or rejects a deprecated
one, they disagree on what's valid. Centralizing the format in this
module means every consumer sees the same parse result; bypassing
it re-decentralizes exactly the surface this module exists to own.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` -- parent root spec; established the two-field
  minimum frontmatter schema (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`), the
  deprecated-field rejection invariant, the `.aide/config/`
  user-owned directory boundary, the substitution-surface partition
  (install-time on `mcpServerConfig.args`, never on body content),
  and the no-server-side-rewrites-of-body-content rule. The Strategy
  paragraph "Brain configuration is user-owned, not code-owned"
  drove the move of hub-template content out of inline package
  constants and into the user-owned `brain.aide`, which is the
  load-bearing reason this module's body grew to three sections.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` -- sibling consumer
  spec; specifies that the playbook-hub and research-hub artifact
  InitSteps source their `content` bytes from the corresponding
  body sections of the scaffolded `brain.aide` via this parser,
  never from inline TypeScript constants. The per-consumer section
  ownership rule (`playbookHub` + `researchHub` only, never `prose`)
  is grounded in this spec's outcomes about hub-artifact byte
  sourcing and the no-inline-template-constants invariant.
- `src/tools/brain/.aide` -- sibling consumer spec; specifies that
  the runtime brain tool reads `prose` only and never reaches into
  any other field of the parsed config on the `ok` branch. The
  per-consumer section ownership rule (`prose` only, never the hub
  sections) is grounded in this spec's
  "frontmatter-is-syncs-input, prose-is-the-agents-input" boundary.
- `src/types/index.ts` -- canonical home of the `BrainAideConfig`,
  `BrainAideMcpServerConfig`, and `ParseBrainAideResult` type
  definitions. The central-types invariant in the Strategy
  paragraph "Types live centrally in `src/types/`" requires the
  parsed-shape additions for `playbookHub` and `researchHub` to
  land here next to the existing `BrainAideConfig` definition,
  imported by every consumer, never re-declared module-private.
